{
  "schema": 1,
  "status": "passed",
  "platform": "macos-arm64-native",
  "qualification": "one fixed 64 MiB unread-output sample per row through the real keeper and each native transport; not throughput, aggregate-memory or denial-of-service qualification",
  "keeper_change": "the session output channel is bounded at 32 frames of at most 8 KiB (256 KiB); a client that holds its connection open without reading now stalls the child through its pipe/pty instead of growing the keeper",
  "vmm_finding": {
    "adapter": "libkrun",
    "defect": "the unix proxy wrote guest stream data to its host socket with a blocking send on the vsock queue thread and returned credit as soon as bytes entered the kernel buffer, so one host client that stopped reading stalled every vsock connection of that VM: other keeper sessions, SSH and the editor",
    "measured": "a new connection to the same VM got SpawnOk only when the unread client resumed reading (3.0 s, the probe's hold); the sibling VM was unaffected (1 ms)",
    "macos_fact": "MSG_DONTWAIT does not make send non-blocking on a blocking AF_UNIX socket on macOS (measured: send blocked until SIGALRM); O_NONBLOCK returns EAGAIN after 8 KiB",
    "patch": {
      "path": "experiments/microvm/patches/libkrun-vsock-host-backpressure.patch",
      "sha256": "67a1e6ea2193dc17a37cdb5965f86b810337c435131440c653aa02576bcced7f",
      "change": "connected unix proxies stay O_NONBLOCK; data the host socket cannot take is queued per connection and flushed on writability; credit (fwd_cnt) advances only as bytes reach the socket, so the queue is bounded by the advertised 8 MiB and a guest that exceeds its credit is reset; guest shutdown/close is deferred until the queue drains",
      "status": "local experimental correction; not upstream merged",
      "first_version_excluded": "the first version relied on MSG_DONTWAIT and still blocked on macOS"
    }
  },
